        "**4. What is the purpose of ANOVA, and how does it differ from a t-test?**\n",
        "\n",
        "Purpose of ANOVA (Analysis of Variance)\n",
        "- ANOVA is used to compare the means of **three or more** groups to determine if at least one group mean is significantly different.  \n",
        "- It analyzes the **variance** within and between groups to test for statistical significance.  \n",
        "- Commonly used in experiments and research studies to compare multiple treatments or conditions.  \n",
        "\n",
        "### **Difference Between ANOVA and t-test**  \n",
        "\n",
        "ANOVA (**Analysis of Variance**) is used when comparing the means of **three or more groups** to determine if at least one group mean is significantly different. It evaluates the **variance** within and between groups using the **F-statistic**. In contrast, a **t-test** is used when comparing the means of **two groups**, testing whether they are significantly different using the **t-statistic**. While ANOVA considers variance across multiple groups, a t-test directly compares means. If only two groups are involved, a **t-test** is sufficient, but for **three or more groups**, **ANOVA** is required to avoid inflating the Type I error rate.\n",
        "\n"
