        "**5. Explain when and why you would use a one-way ANOVA instead of multiple t-tests when comparing more than two group.**\n",
        "\n",
        "When to Use One-Way ANOVA:\n",
        "- When comparing three or more independent groups.  \n",
        "- When the dependent variable is continuous , and the groups are categorical.  \n",
        "- When assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variance are met.  \n",
        "\n",
        "Why do we use One-Way ANOVA Instead of Multiple t-Tests\n",
        "1. **Controls Type I Error (False Positives)** – Conducting multiple t-tests increases the risk of incorrectly rejecting the null hypothesis (Type I error). One-way ANOVA controls this by testing all group differences in a single test.  \n",
        "2. **More Efficient** – Instead of running multiple t-tests, ANOVA provides a single test to determine if there is a significant difference among groups.  \n",
        "3. **Compares All Groups Simultaneously** – ANOVA considers all group variances together, giving a broader picture of differences rather than just pairwise comparisons.  \n",
        "4. **Leads to Further Post Hoc Analysis** – If ANOVA detects a significant difference, post hoc tests (e.g., Tukey’s HSD) can determine which specific groups  differ without inflating errors.  "
