        "**6. Explain how variance is partitioned in ANOVA into between-group variance and within-group variance.How does this partitioning contribute to the calculation of the F-statistic?**\n",
        "\n",
        "In ANOVA, the total variance in the data is divided into two components:  \n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Between-Group Variance (Explained Variance)**  \n",
        "   - Measures the variation **between** different group means.  \n",
        "   - Captures how much the group means differ from the overall mean.  \n",
        "   - Represents variability due to the effect of the independent variable (treatment or condition).  \n",
        "\n",
        "2. **Within-Group Variance (Unexplained Variance)**  \n",
        "   - Measures the variation **within** each group.  \n",
        "   - Represents random variation or individual differences that are **not** due to the independent variable.  \n",
        "\n",
        "Contribution to the F-Statistic Calculation\n",
        "\n",
        "The **F-statistic** in ANOVA is calculated as the ratio of between-group variance to within-group variance:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\[\n",
        "F = \\frac{\\text{Between-Group Variance}}{\\text{Within-Group Variance}}\n",
        "\\]\n",
        "\n",
        "- A **higher F-value** suggests that the differences between groups are larger than the variation within groups, indicating a significant effect of the independent variable.  \n",
        "- A **lower F-value** suggests that the differences between groups are small compared to within-group variation, meaning no significant effect."
