        "**7. Compare the classical (frequentist) approach to ANOVA with the Bayesian approach. What are the key differences in terms of how they handle uncertainty, parameter estimation, and hypothesis testing?**\n",
        "\n",
        "Classical ANOVA, based on the frequentist approach, tests whether there is a significant difference among group means using an F-statistic and p-values. It assumes fixed but unknown population parameters and relies on long-run frequency properties of data. A significant p-value (typically < 0.05) leads to rejecting the null hypothesis, indicating at least one group mean differs. However, it does not provide the probability of hypotheses being true, only whether the observed data is unlikely under the null hypothesis.  \n",
        "\n",
        "Bayesian ANOVA, in contrast, estimates the probability distribution of parameters using prior beliefs and observed data. Instead of p-values, it computes posterior distributions and Bayes factors, allowing for probabilistic interpretations. Bayesian methods are more flexible, incorporating prior knowledge and handling small or imbalanced datasets better. Unlike frequentist ANOVA, which provides a binary decision, Bayesian ANOVA gives a full range of possible values for group means and their uncertainties.  \n"
